window.ACADEMY_SEARCH = [{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 1","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"arithmetic\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 2","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · How big is that, really?","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-num\">1 How big is that, really?"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 4","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 5","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-body\"> The most useful arithmetic skill is not calculation — it is estimation . The physicist Enrico Fermi used to ask his students questions like \"how many piano tuners are in Chicago?\" and get answers within a factor "},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 6","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"arithmetic\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 7","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Ratios, proportions, percentages","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-num\">2 Ratios, proportions, percentages"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 9","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 10","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-body\"> Percentages are the native language of modern deception, and the defense is one question: percent of what ? \"Prices rose 50%!\" — of what base? \"Risk doubled!\" — from 1 in a million to 2 in a million? A percentage"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 11","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"arithmetic\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 12","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Mental math that actually works","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-num\">3 Mental math that actually works"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 14","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 15","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-body\"> The Greeks did serious mathematics without a single written numeral for zero — mental manipulation was the whole game. Three tricks cover 90% of daily life. Split numbers: 8 × 47 = (8 × 40) + (8 × 7) = 320 + 56 ="},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 16","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"arithmetic\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 17","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Proof: why the primes never end","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-num\">4 Proof: why the primes never end"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 19","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 20","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-body\"> A proof is an argument that settles a question forever — not by evidence or authority, but by pure reasoning. Around 300 BC, Euclid proved there are infinitely many prime numbers, and his proof still works today,"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 21","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"arithmetic\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 22","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Statistics literacy","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-num\">5 Statistics literacy"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 24","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 25","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-body\"> The ancients distrusted rhetoric; the modern equivalent is distrusting statistics presented without context . Three defenses cover most cases. Mean vs. median: the mean is dragged by outliers — a town where nine "},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 26","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"arithmetic\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 27","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Capstone: Fermi problems and a stats teardown","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: Fermi problems and a stats teardown"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 29","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Arithmetic · Lesson 30","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"-body\"> Two tasks. No calculator for the first. Three Fermi problems (estimate each, showing your piece-by-piece reasoning): how many gallons of paint to cover every house on your street? How many words have you spoken i"},{"t":"Arithmetic — course overview","u":"courses/arithmetic.html","s":"Quadrivium I Arithmetic The art of number. Not long division — number sense : knowing roughly how big things are, doing math in your head, and refusing to be fooled by figures. 1 How big is that, really? ▾ The most usefu"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 1","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"astronomy\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 2","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · The naked-eye sky","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-num\">1 The naked-eye sky"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 4","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 5","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-body\"> Before telescopes, there was the oldest instrument of all: patient eyes. The Greeks noticed that most stars are fixed in their patterns, but five \"stars\" wander — planetes , Greek for wanderers. We still call the"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 6","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"astronomy\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 7","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Greek models: Eudoxus to Ptolemy","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-num\">2 Greek models: Eudoxus to Ptolemy"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 9","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 10","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-body\"> The planets don't just wander — they sometimes loop backward ( retrograde motion ) before resuming course. Eudoxus (4th century BC) modeled this with nested spinning spheres; Ptolemy (2nd century AD) refined it i"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 11","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"astronomy\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 12","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · The Copernican turn and Kepler","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-num\">3 The Copernican turn and Kepler"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 14","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 15","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-body\"> Copernicus (1543) moved the sun to the center — not because the data demanded it yet, but because the resulting system was simpler : retrograde motion falls out naturally when a faster inner planet (Earth) laps a"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 16","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"astronomy\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 17","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · The scale of things","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-num\">4 The scale of things"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 19","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 20","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-body\"> Around 240 BC, Eratosthenes measured the Earth's circumference with a stick and a well (see the Geometry capstone): about 39,000 km — within a few percent of the true 40,075 km. From there the ladder climbs: the "},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 21","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"astronomy\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 22","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Modern cosmology, honestly","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-num\">5 Modern cosmology, honestly"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 24","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 25","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-body\"> The current story, stated plainly: the universe is expanding — galaxies recede from each other, and the farther, the faster. Run the film backward and everything converges ~13.8 billion years ago in a hot, dense "},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 26","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"astronomy\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 27","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Astronomy · Capstone: five constellations and the view from above","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: five constellations and the view from above"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 29","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Astronomy · Lesson 30","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"-body\"> Two tasks — one outward, one inward. The Greeks always paired them. Find five constellations with your own eyes (apps may confirm, not replace): the Big Dipper, Cassiopeia, Orion, Leo, and Scorpius — or any five "},{"t":"Astronomy — course overview","u":"courses/astronomy.html","s":"Quadrivium IV Astronomy The art of the cosmos. The Greeks measured the Earth with a stick and mapped the heavens with geometry — and their science doubled as philosophy. 1 The naked-eye sky ▾ Before telescopes, there was"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 1","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"\" data-course=\"geometry\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 2","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Euclid's game","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-num\">1 Euclid's game"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 4","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 5","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-body\"> Euclid's genius wasn't discovering geometry — much of it was already known. It was organizing it: start from a handful of agreed starting points, and derive everything else by pure logic. The starting points are "},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 6","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"\" data-course=\"geometry\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 7","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Triangles and Pythagoras","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-num\">2 Triangles and Pythagoras"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 9","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 10","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-body\"> The triangle is geometry's atom: any polygon can be cut into triangles, so triangle facts become all shape facts. The king of them: a² + b² = c² for a right triangle — the square on the hypotenuse equals the sum "},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 11","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"\" data-course=\"geometry\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 12","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Circles and pi","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-num\">3 Circles and pi"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 14","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 15","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-body\"> Measure any circle — a coin, a wheel, a plate — and divide its circumference by its diameter. You always get the same number: π ≈ 3.14159… That constancy is a genuine wonder: every circle in the universe obeys th"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 16","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"\" data-course=\"geometry\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 17","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Area, volume, and the tyranny of scale","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-num\">4 Area, volume, and the tyranny of scale"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 19","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 20","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-body\"> Formulas are easier to keep if you build them: a rectangle's area (length × width) cut diagonally gives two triangles — so triangle area is half the rectangle's. A circle rearranged into wedges gives πr² (last le"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 21","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"\" data-course=\"geometry\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 22","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Similarity and scale: measuring the unreachable","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-num\">5 Similarity and scale: measuring the unreachable"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 24","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 25","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-body\"> Legend says Thales measured the Great Pyramid's height by waiting until his own shadow equaled his height — at that moment, the pyramid's shadow equaled the pyramid's height. The principle is similarity : same sh"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 26","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"geometry\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 27","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Geometry · Capstone: Eratosthenes-style measurement","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: Eratosthenes-style measurement"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 29","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Geometry · Lesson 30","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"-body\"> Around 240 BC, Eratosthenes measured the Earth's circumference with a stick, a well, and geometry: at noon on the summer solstice the sun shone straight down a well in Syene, while in Alexandria a stick cast a sh"},{"t":"Geometry — course overview","u":"courses/geometry.html","s":"Quadrivium II Geometry The art of proof and space. Euclid's Elements was the most-used textbook in history — two thousand years of students learned to think by proving things about triangles. 1 Euclid's game ▾ Euclid's g"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 1","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"\" data-course=\"grammar\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 2","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Why grammar comes first","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-num\">1 Why grammar comes first"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 4","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 5","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-body\"> The Romans put grammatica first because every other art depends on it: you cannot evaluate an argument you cannot parse, and you cannot learn from a book you read sloppily. The modern reading researcher Mortimer "},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 6","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"\" data-course=\"grammar\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 7","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Words: the Latin and Greek inside English","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-num\">2 Words: the Latin and Greek inside English"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 9","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 10","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-body\"> Over half of English vocabulary comes from Latin (directly or through French) and Greek — and nearly all of its abstract vocabulary does. Learn twenty roots and thousands of words unlock themselves. Root From Mea"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 11","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"\" data-course=\"grammar\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 12","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Sentences: structure is meaning","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-num\">3 Sentences: structure is meaning"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 14","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 15","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-body\"> Every sentence is a small machine: a subject doing something (verb), usually to something (object), with modifiers bolted on. Most confusion in reading comes from losing track of the machine inside long sentences"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 16","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"\" data-course=\"grammar\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 17","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Latin primer I: how the language works","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-num\">4 Latin primer I: how the language works"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 19","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 20","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-body\"> Latin is worth meeting even if you never become fluent: it trains precision, unlocks Romance languages, and lets you read two thousand years of inscriptions, mottos, and law in the original. The core idea: Latin "},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 21","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"\" data-course=\"grammar\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 22","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Latin primer II: reading real Latin","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-num\">5 Latin primer II: reading real Latin"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 24","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 25","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-body\"> More essential vocabulary: sapientia (wisdom), virtus (excellence/virtue), fortuna (fortune/luck), ratio (reason), lex (law), populus (people), senatus (senate), bellum (war), pax (peace), libertas (freedom). Now"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 26","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"grammar\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 27","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Grammar · Capstone: parse and translate","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: parse and translate"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 29","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Grammar · Lesson 30","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"-body\"> Two tasks. Take your time — this is the whole course in miniature. Parse: choose any paragraph of serious prose (an essay, a speech, a contract). Reduce every sentence to its skeleton and write down exactly what "},{"t":"Grammar — course overview","u":"courses/grammar.html","s":"Trivium I Grammar The art of reading well. Before you can reason about a text or argue from it, you must be able to take it apart and see how it's built. 1 Why grammar comes first ▾ The Romans put grammatica first becaus"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 1","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"\" data-course=\"greek\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 2","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Greek I · The alphabet: twenty-four letters","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-num\">1 The alphabet: twenty-four letters"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 4","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 5","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-body\"> Greek writes left to right, like English, and many capitals will look familiar — the Roman alphabet was borrowed from Greek. There are 24 letters. Learn their names; classicists refer to letters by name the way m"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 6","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"\" data-course=\"greek\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 7","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Breathings, accents, and diphthongs","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-num\">2 Breathings, accents, and diphthongs"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 9","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 10","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-body\"> Three small marks turn letter-sounds into real words. Breathings. Every word beginning with a vowel (or rho) carries a breathing mark. The rough breathing (῾) adds an h : ἁρμονία is \"harmonia.\" The smooth breathi"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 11","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"\" data-course=\"greek\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 12","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Nouns: the second declension","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-num\">3 Nouns: the second declension"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 14","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 15","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-body\"> Like Latin (see Grammar, lesson 4), Greek marks a noun's job with its ending. Three cases do most of the work: the nominative (subject — who acts), the accusative (object — who is acted upon), and the genitive (p"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 16","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"\" data-course=\"greek\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 17","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Verbs: the present tense and εἰμί","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-num\">4 Verbs: the present tense and εἰμί"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 19","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 20","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-body\"> Greek verbs are machines: a stem (the meaning) plus an ending (who does it). Most verbs follow the -ω conjugation . Take λυ- (loose, free) + endings: Singular Plural 1st (I / we) λύω λύομεν 2nd (you) λύεις λύετε "},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 21","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"\" data-course=\"greek\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 22","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Sixty words and the Greek inside English","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-num\">5 Sixty words and the Greek inside English"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 24","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 25","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-body\"> Vocabulary is the long labor of any language, but sixty well-chosen words plus the endings you now know will let you start reading real sentences. Learn them in groups — the brain keeps labeled shelves better tha"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 26","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"greek\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 27","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Greek I · Capstone: your first Greek reading","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: your first Greek reading"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 29","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Greek I · Lesson 30","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"-body\"> Two real texts. Read each aloud first (lesson 2's rules), then work through the gloss with the grammar you now own. 1. Xenophon, Anabasis , opening (slightly simplified). In 401 BC Xenophon marched with ten thous"},{"t":"Greek I — course overview","u":"courses/greek.html","s":"Tongues II Greek I Learn to read Ancient Greek — the language of Homer, Plato, and the New Testament. Six lessons from the alphabet to your first real texts. 1 The alphabet: twenty-four letters ▾ Greek writes left to rig"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 1","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"\" data-course=\"gymnastics\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 2","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · The Greek ideal","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-num\">1 The Greek ideal"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 4","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 5","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-body\"> Greek paideia — the formation of a person — had two wings: music (everything we now call the humanities) for the soul, and gymnastics for the body. Boys trained daily in the palaestra; athletes were civic heroes;"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 6","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"\" data-course=\"gymnastics\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 7","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Movement foundations: the five patterns","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-num\">2 Movement foundations: the five patterns"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 9","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 10","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-body\"> All human strength reduces to five movement patterns . Master these unloaded and you've built the base for everything else: Squat — knees track over toes, chest up, full depth if your joints allow. The pattern of"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 11","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"\" data-course=\"gymnastics\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 12","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Walking and running: the ancient base","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-num\">3 Walking and running: the ancient base"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 14","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 15","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-body\"> The Greeks walked everywhere — between cities, to the gymnasium, while philosophizing. Walking was the aerobic base of ancient life, and modern physiology confirms the prescription: zone 2 cardio (a pace where yo"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 16","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"\" data-course=\"gymnastics\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 17","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Strength without a gym","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-num\">4 Strength without a gym"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 19","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 20","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-body\"> Strength grows by progressive overload : gradually demanding slightly more than last time. No gym required — bodyweight offers built-in progressions , rungs on a ladder: Push: wall push-up → incline (counter) → f"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 21","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"\" data-course=\"gymnastics\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 22","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Recovery: sleep and simple food","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-num\">5 Recovery: sleep and simple food"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 24","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 25","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-body\"> The ancient athletes understood what modern science keeps rediscovering: you don't get stronger from training — you get stronger from recovering from training. The workout is the stimulus; sleep and food are the "},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 26","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"gymnastics\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 27","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Capstone: your pentathlon","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: your pentathlon"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 29","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Gymnastics · Lesson 30","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"-body\"> The ancient pentathlon tested the complete athlete: running, jumping, discus, javelin, wrestling. Yours tests the complete you — five baseline events, no equipment beyond a timer: Brisk 1-mile walk or run — time "},{"t":"Gymnastics — course overview","u":"courses/gymnastics.html","s":"The Foundation Gymnastics Training the body. The Greeks schooled mind and body together from childhood — a neglected body was an unfinished education. 1 The Greek ideal ▾ Greek paideia — the formation of a person — had t"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 1","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"\" data-course=\"latin\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 2","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Sound: the alphabet, classical pronunciation, and stress","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-num\">1 Sound: the alphabet, classical pronunciation, and stress"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 4","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 5","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-body\"> Latin uses the alphabet you're reading now — because this alphabet is the Latin alphabet, borrowed by English. Classical Latin had 23 letters: no j (written i ), and u and v were one letter ( v ). The letters k ,"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 6","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"\" data-course=\"latin\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 7","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Nouns: the 1st and 2nd declensions, nominative and accusative","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-num\">2 Nouns: the 1st and 2nd declensions, nominative and accusative"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 9","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 10","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-body\"> A Latin noun changes its ending to show its case — its job in the sentence. Two cases do most of the work: Nominative: the subject (who acts). Puella — \"the girl\" (as subject). Accusative: the direct object (who "},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 11","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"\" data-course=\"latin\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 12","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Verbs: the present tense, 1st conjugation, and esse","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-num\">3 Verbs: the present tense, 1st conjugation, and esse"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 14","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 15","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-body\"> Latin verbs don't need pronouns: the ending is the pronoun. The 1st conjugation (the -āre family, e.g. amāre , to love) conjugates like this in the present: Person Singular Plural 1st (I / we) am ō — I love am ām"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 16","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"\" data-course=\"latin\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 17","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Building sentences: word order, adjectives, prepositions","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-num\">4 Building sentences: word order, adjectives, prepositions"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 19","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 20","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-body\"> Latin's default word order is subject – object – verb : Puella servum vocat (\"The girl the slave calls\"). The verb goes last; the first and last positions are the emphatic ones, so Romans moved words there for st"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 21","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"\" data-course=\"latin\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 22","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Core vocabulary: the 60 words that unlock Latin — and Latin alive in English","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-num\">5 Core vocabulary: the 60 words that unlock Latin — and Latin alive in English"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 24","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 25","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-body\"> Frequency beats completeness: a small core of words appears in most Latin you'll ever read. Grammar's lesson 2 gave you the roots hiding inside English — now meet the Latin words themselves. Nouns (20): Latin Mea"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 26","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"latin\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 27","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Latin I · Capstone: Caesar's Gaul and a real Roman inscription","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: Caesar's Gaul and a real Roman inscription"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 29","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Latin I · Lesson 30","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"-body\"> Two translations. Use everything from lessons 1–5; every word you haven't met is glossed. 1. Caesar, simplified. Julius Caesar opened his account of the Gallic Wars with the most famous first sentence in Latin pr"},{"t":"Latin I — course overview","u":"courses/latin.html","s":"Tongues I Latin I Learn to read Latin — the language of Rome, of law, and of science. 1 Sound: the alphabet, classical pronunciation, and stress ▾ Latin uses the alphabet you're reading now — because this alphabet is the"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 1","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"logic\">"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 2","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Logic · Arguments: premises, conclusions, and the two questions","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-num\">1 Arguments: premises, conclusions, and the two questions"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 4","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 5","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-body\"> An argument is a set of statements where some (premises) are offered as support for another (the conclusion). Two questions, in order: Is it valid? — If the premises were true, would the conclusion have to be tru"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 6","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"logic\">"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 7","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Logic · The syllogism and its traps","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-num\">2 The syllogism and its traps"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 9","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 10","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-body\"> Aristotle's syllogism is the atom of deductive reasoning: All humans are mortal. Socrates is human. Therefore Socrates is mortal. Two classic traps to memorize: Affirming the consequent: \"If it rains, the ground "},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 11","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"logic\">"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 12","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Logic · Fallacies I: attacks on the arguer","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-num\">3 Fallacies I: attacks on the arguer"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 14","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 15","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-body\"> Informal fallacies are bad moves that look persuasive. First family: instead of engaging the argument, attack something near it. Ad hominem: \"You can't trust his tax plan — he's a convicted fraudster.\" Maybe true"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 16","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"logic\">"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 17","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Logic · Fallacies II: tricks with the evidence","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-num\">4 Fallacies II: tricks with the evidence"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 19","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 20","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-body\"> Second family: the argument looks evidence-based, but the evidence doesn't do the claimed work. Cherry-picking: citing only the data that fits. The antidote: ask what the full dataset shows. False dilemma: \"Eithe"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 21","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"\" data-course=\"logic\">"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 22","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Logic · Beyond deduction: induction and abduction","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-num\">5 Beyond deduction: induction and abduction"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 24","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 25","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-body\"> Most real reasoning isn't deductive. Two other modes run daily life: Induction: from observed cases to general rules. \"Every swan I've seen is white → all swans are white.\" Powerful, never certain — one black swa"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 26","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"logic\">"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 27","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Logic · Capstone: the fact-checker's teardown","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: the fact-checker's teardown"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 29","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Logic · Lesson 30","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"-body\"> Pick one opinion piece or viral video making a contested claim. Produce a one-page teardown: State the conclusion in one sentence (in your own words — no strawmanning). List the premises. Mark each: verified / un"},{"t":"Logic — course overview","u":"courses/logic.html","s":"Trivium II Logic The art of thinking well. Arguments have structure; most public reasoning collapses the moment you check the structure. 1 Arguments: premises, conclusions, and the two questions ▾ An argument is a set of"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 1","u":"courses/music.html","s":"\" data-course=\"music\">"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 2","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Music · Sound and the harmonic series","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-num\">1 Sound and the harmonic series"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 4","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 5","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-body\"> Sound is air vibrating. Frequency — vibrations per second (hertz) — is what we hear as pitch: faster vibration, higher note. But no real note is a single frequency. Pluck a string and it vibrates as a whole and i"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 6","u":"courses/music.html","s":"\" data-course=\"music\">"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 7","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Music · Intervals and ratios: Pythagoras's discovery","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-num\">2 Intervals and ratios: Pythagoras's discovery"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 9","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 10","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-body\"> Legend says Pythagoras passed a blacksmith's shop, heard hammers ringing in harmony, and traced it to simple weight ratios. The cleaner story: he divided a monochord (a single string) and found that the beautiful"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 11","u":"courses/music.html","s":"\" data-course=\"music\">"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 12","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Music · Scales and modes","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-num\">3 Scales and modes"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 14","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 15","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-body\"> A scale is a ladder of notes; a mode is which rung you treat as home. Play the white keys from C to C and you get the major scale — bright, resolved. Play the same white keys from A to A and you get the natural m"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 16","u":"courses/music.html","s":"\" data-course=\"music\">"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 17","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Music · Rhythm and meter","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-num\">4 Rhythm and meter"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 19","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 20","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-body\"> Beat is the pulse; rhythm is what you do with it. Meter groups beats: 4/4 (four beats per measure — most pop, rock, and marches), 3/4 (three — waltzes), 6/8 (a rolling two — Irish jigs, \"We Are the Champions\" ver"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 21","u":"courses/music.html","s":"\" data-course=\"music\">"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 22","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Music · How to really listen","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-num\">5 How to really listen"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 24","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 25","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-body\"> Most listening is background; active listening is a skill. The key concept is form — how a piece is built. Almost all popular music runs on repetition with variation : verse, chorus, verse, chorus, bridge, chorus"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 26","u":"courses/music.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"music\">"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 27","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Music · Capstone: analyze and compose","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: analyze and compose"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 29","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Music · Lesson 30","u":"courses/music.html","s":"-body\"> Two tasks — one of reception, one of creation. No instrument needed. Full structure analysis: pick one song and write a complete map — timestamped sections, the mode (major/minor), the meter, the moment of maximu"},{"t":"Music — course overview","u":"courses/music.html","s":"Quadrivium III Music The math of beauty. The Greeks put music in the quadrivium — with arithmetic and geometry — because harmony is number made audible. 1 Sound and the harmonic series ▾ Sound is air vibrating. Frequency"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 1","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"philosophy\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 2","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · The question","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-num\">1 The question"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 4","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 5","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-body\"> Every Greek school was an answer to one question: what is the good life? Their word was eudaimonia — usually translated \"happiness,\" but it means something sturdier: flourishing , living well, a life that works. "},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 6","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"philosophy\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 7","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Socrates and the examined life","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-num\">2 Socrates and the examined life"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 9","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 10","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-body\"> Socrates wrote nothing. He walked Athens asking questions — the elenchus , or cross-examination: take someone's confident claim, ask what it means, follow the implications, and watch the confidence dissolve into "},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 11","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"philosophy\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 12","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Plato in brief","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-num\">3 Plato in brief"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 14","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 15","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-body\"> Plato, Socrates's student, built the first complete system. Two ideas carry the weight. The tripartite soul: reason (the charioteer), spirit (ambition, anger — the noble horse), and appetite (hunger, lust — the u"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 16","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"philosophy\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 17","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Aristotle: virtue as habit","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-num\">4 Aristotle: virtue as habit"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 19","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 20","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-body\"> Aristotle, Plato's student, brought philosophy down to earth: we become just by doing just acts, brave by doing brave acts — virtue is not a theory but a habit , built the way a muscle is built, by repetition. Yo"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 21","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"\" data-course=\"philosophy\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 22","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Stoicism: the system","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-num\">5 Stoicism: the system"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 24","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 25","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-body\"> Stoicism is the most usable Greek philosophy — a complete operating system for difficult times. Three core mechanisms. The dichotomy of control: some things are up to us (judgments, intentions, responses), everyt"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 26","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"philosophy\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 27","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Philosophy · Capstone: your philosophy of life","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: your philosophy of life"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 29","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Philosophy · Lesson 30","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"-body\"> The whole academy converges here. Write your one-page personal philosophy — not what sounds impressive, what you'll actually live by: What the good life is — your answer to lesson 1's question, revised after six "},{"t":"Philosophy — course overview","u":"courses/philosophy.html","s":"The Crown Philosophy The love of wisdom — and the crown of the curriculum. Every other art trained your faculties; this one asks what they're for . 1 The question ▾ Every Greek school was an answer to one question: what "},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 1","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"\" data-course=\"rhetoric\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 2","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · The five canons","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-num\">1 The five canons"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 4","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 5","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-body\"> Classical rhetoric had five canons — five jobs every speaker must do: Invention ( inventio ): finding what to say — discovering arguments. Arrangement ( dispositio ): ordering it for maximum clarity and force. St"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 6","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"\" data-course=\"rhetoric\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 7","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Invention: finding arguments","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-num\">2 Invention: finding arguments"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 9","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 10","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-body\"> The Greeks didn't wait for inspiration; they had topics ( topoi ) — places to look for arguments. Run any position through these: Definition: what exactly are we talking about? (Half of all disputes are definitio"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 11","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"\" data-course=\"rhetoric\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 12","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Arrangement: the classical speech","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-num\">3 Arrangement: the classical speech"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 14","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 15","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-body\"> Cicero's six-part structure still underlies every effective persuasive piece — essays, videos, pitches: Exordium — the opening: win attention and goodwill. Start with the stakes, not throat-clearing. Narratio — t"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 16","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"\" data-course=\"rhetoric\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 17","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Style: clarity is the virtue","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-num\">4 Style: clarity is the virtue"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 19","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 20","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-body\"> The ancients named three levels of style — plain (teach), middle (please), grand (move) — and the master knew when each was due. For almost all modern purposes, the plain style wins. Its rules: Concrete over abst"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 21","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"\" data-course=\"rhetoric\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 22","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Ethos, pathos, logos — in that order","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-num\">5 Ethos, pathos, logos — in that order"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 24","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 25","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-body\"> Aristotle's three appeals are usually listed logos-first. In practice, audiences process them in reverse order of prestige: Ethos (character): Should I trust this person? Decided in the first minute — from honest"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 26","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"\" data-course=\"rhetoric\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 27","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Memory and delivery: the method of loci","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-num\">6 Memory and delivery: the method of loci"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 29","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 30","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-body\"> Roman orators spoke for hours without notes using the method of loci : associate each point with a location along a familiar route (your house, your walk to work), in order. To recall, mentally walk the route. It"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 31","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"capstone\" data-course=\"rhetoric\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 32","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-head\">"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Capstone: the three-minute speech","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-num\">★ Capstone: the three-minute speech"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 34","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-toggle\">▾"},{"t":"Rhetoric · Lesson 35","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"-body\"> Write and deliver (aloud, to a person or a camera) a three-minute persuasive speech: Use the six-part arrangement. Include a refutatio — answer your strongest objection honestly. One tricolon or anaphora, no more"},{"t":"Rhetoric — course overview","u":"courses/rhetoric.html","s":"Trivium III Rhetoric The art of speaking well. Logic finds the truth; rhetoric carries it to other minds intact. 1 The five canons ▾ Classical rhetoric had five canons — five jobs every speaker must do: Invention ( inven"},{"t":"Library · Meditations","u":"library.html#meditations","s":"card\" id=\"meditations\"> Meditations Marcus Aurelius, Emperor of Rome · c. 170–180 CE · trans. George Long (1862) Why read it — A Roman emperor's private notebook, written in Greek on campaign along the Danube and never m"},{"t":"Library · Enchiridion","u":"library.html#enchiridion","s":"card\" id=\"enchiridion\"> Enchiridion Epictetus (compiled by Arrian) · c. 100 CE · trans. Thomas Wentworth Higginson Why read it — The shortest complete Stoic manual: 53 chapters you can read in an hour and practice for a "},{"t":"Library · Apology","u":"library.html#apology","s":"card\" id=\"apology\"> Apology Plato · 399 BCE (dramatic date) · trans. Benjamin Jowett (1871) Why read it — Socrates' defense speech at his trial for corrupting the youth. The founding document of the examined life — and a"},{"t":"Library · Nicomachean Ethics (selections)","u":"library.html#ethics","s":"card\" id=\"ethics\"> Nicomachean Ethics (selections) Aristotle · c. 350 BCE · trans. W. D. Ross Why read it — The most influential account of what a good human life is: happiness as activity rather than feeling, virtue as "},{"t":"Library · Euclid's Elements, Book I (selections)","u":"library.html#euclid","s":"card\" id=\"euclid\"> Euclid's Elements, Book I (selections) Euclid · c. 300 BCE · ed. John Casey (1885) Why read it — The model of deductive reasoning for two thousand years. From a handful of definitions and axioms, Book "},{"t":"Library · Antigone","u":"library.html#antigone","s":"card\" id=\"antigone\"> Antigone Sophocles · c. 441 BCE · trans. R. C. Jebb Why read it — The tragedy of two rights in collision: Antigone buries her brother in obedience to the unwritten laws of the gods; Creon forbids it "},{"t":"Library · Parallel Lives (selections)","u":"library.html#plutarch","s":"card\" id=\"plutarch\"> Parallel Lives (selections) Plutarch · c. 100 CE · trans. Aubrey Stewart and George Long Why read it — Biography as moral education. Plutarch pairs Greek and Roman lives — Theseus with Romulus, Solon"},{"t":"Library · On Duties (selections)","u":"library.html#duties","s":"card\" id=\"duties\"> On Duties (selections) Cicero · 44 BCE · trans. Walter Miller (1913) Why read it — Rome's handbook for public life, written in the last year of Cicero's life for his son. How the honorable and the usef"},{"t":"Library · On the Nature of Things (selections)","u":"library.html#lucretius","s":"card\" id=\"lucretius\"> On the Nature of Things (selections) Lucretius · c. 55 BCE · trans. William Ellery Leonard (1916) Why read it — An Epicurean poem with the boldest thesis of antiquity: the universe is atoms and void"},{"t":"Library · Histories (selections)","u":"library.html#herodotus","s":"card\" id=\"herodotus\"> Histories (selections) Herodotus · c. 440 BCE · trans. G. C. Macaulay (1890) Why read it — The first history in Western literature: an inquiry into why Greeks and barbarians went to war, told throug"},{"t":"Timeline · 776 BC The first Olympic Games","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> 776 BC The first Olympic Games Tradition dates the first Olympiad here. The Greeks begin counting time in four-year Olympiads — and gymnastics becomes a civic institution."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 750 BC Homer's Iliad and Odyssey","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> c. 750 BC Homer's Iliad and Odyssey The epics take written shape. For a thousand years they are the Greek schoolbook: every educated child learns to read on Homer."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 700 BC Hesiod's Theogony","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> c. 700 BC Hesiod's Theogony The gods get a family tree — and ordinary farming life gets its poem in the Works and Days ."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 585 BC Thales of Miletus","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> c. 585 BC Thales of Miletus Thales predicts a solar eclipse and argues water is the first principle. Philosophy — explaining the world without gods — begins."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 530 BC Pythagoras","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> c. 530 BC Pythagoras In Croton, Pythagoras founds a brotherhood where number is the essence of reality. Music and arithmetic fuse: harmony is ratio."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 500 BC Heraclitus and Parmenides","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> c. 500 BC Heraclitus and Parmenides Heraclitus: everything flows. Parmenides: change is illusion, argued in strict verse. Logic is born from their quarrel."},{"t":"Timeline · 594 BC Solon's reforms","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> 594 BC Solon's reforms Solon cancels debts, frees debt-slaves, and gives Athens laws it can read. The idea that law binds rulers too takes root."},{"t":"Timeline · 508 BC Democracy in Athens","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"archaic\"> 508 BC Democracy in Athens Cleisthenes reorganizes Athens around ten tribes and the assembly. Ordinary citizens will now need rhetoric — persuasion becomes a civic art."},{"t":"Timeline · 490 BC Marathon","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 490 BC Marathon Athens defeats Persia against the odds. The victory funds a cultural explosion — and the legend of the long run."},{"t":"Timeline · 480 BC Thermopylae and Salamis","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 480 BC Thermopylae and Salamis Leonidas holds the pass; Themistocles wins at sea. Greece survives the Persian Empire."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 470–399 BC Socrates","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> c. 470–399 BC Socrates He writes nothing, questions everything, and teaches Athens to examine itself — dialectic as a way of life."},{"t":"Timeline · 447–432 BC The Parthenon","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 447–432 BC The Parthenon Pericles rebuilds Athens in marble. The Parthenon's proportions are geometry made civic."},{"t":"Timeline · 441 BC Sophocles' Antigone","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 441 BC Sophocles' Antigone A girl buries her brother against the king's edict: the first great dramatization of conscience versus law."},{"t":"Timeline · 431–404 BC The Peloponnesian War","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 431–404 BC The Peloponnesian War Athens and Sparta tear Greece apart. Thucydides writes its history — the first work of political realism."},{"t":"Timeline · 399 BC Socrates executed","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 399 BC Socrates executed Athens condemns its gadfly for corrupting the youth. Plato never forgives the democracy — and writes the Apology ."},{"t":"Timeline · 387 BC Plato founds the Academy","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 387 BC Plato founds the Academy The first university: mathematics required at the door (\"let no one ignorant of geometry enter\"). It will run for 900 years."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 380 BC Plato's Republic","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> c. 380 BC Plato's Republic Justice in the soul, justice in the city — and the allegory of the cave, still the sharpest image of education ever drawn."},{"t":"Timeline · 335 BC Aristotle founds the Lyceum","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"classical\"> 335 BC Aristotle founds the Lyceum Plato's student builds the rival school: logic systematized, biology observed, ethics grounded in habit. The Organon rules reasoning for two millennia."},{"t":"Timeline · 336–323 BC Alexander's conquests","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"hellenistic\"> 336–323 BC Alexander's conquests In thirteen years Alexander carries Greek language and cities from Egypt to India. The Hellenistic world — Greek culture without the polis — begins."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 300 BC Euclid's Elements","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"hellenistic\"> c. 300 BC Euclid's Elements In Alexandria, Euclid assembles the thirteen books of geometry — the most studied textbook in history."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 300 BC Zeno's Stoa","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"hellenistic\"> c. 300 BC Zeno's Stoa Zeno teaches in the Painted Porch at Athens. Stoicism is born: virtue as the only good, the cosmos as one city."},{"t":"Timeline · 306 BC Epicurus' Garden","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"hellenistic\"> 306 BC Epicurus' Garden Epicurus buys a house and teaches that pleasure — understood as the absence of pain — is the goal. The great rival of the Stoa."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 280 BC The Library of Alexandria","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"hellenistic\"> c. 280 BC The Library of Alexandria The Ptolemies gather the world's books — perhaps half a million scrolls. Scholarship becomes an institution."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 250 BC Archimedes","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"hellenistic\"> c. 250 BC Archimedes In Syracuse, Archimedes computes pi, invents the science of levers and buoyancy, and nearly invents calculus."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 150 BC Hipparchus","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"hellenistic\"> c. 150 BC Hipparchus The greatest Greek astronomer : star catalog, precession of the equinoxes, trigonometry for the heavens."},{"t":"Timeline · 146 BC Rome conquers Greece","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> 146 BC Rome conquers Greece Corinth falls. As Horace will put it: captive Greece takes her savage captor captive — Roman elites now learn Greek."},{"t":"Timeline · 44 BC Caesar assassinated","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> 44 BC Caesar assassinated The Ides of March. Cicero, the republic's voice, writes his philosophical works in the wreckage — and is proscribed for it."},{"t":"Timeline · 27 BC Augustus and the Pax Romana","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> 27 BC Augustus and the Pax Romana Octavian becomes Augustus. Two centuries of relative peace let law, roads, and Latin literature flourish — Virgil, Horace, Ovid."},{"t":"Timeline · c. 50 AD Epictetus and the Enchiridion","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> c. 50 AD Epictetus and the Enchiridion A freed slave teaches Stoicism at Nicopolis; his student Arrian writes it down. The handbook of inner freedom."},{"t":"Timeline · 79 AD Vesuvius buries Pompeii","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> 79 AD Vesuvius buries Pompeii A Roman city frozen mid-day: graffiti, bread in ovens, inscriptions — Latin as people actually wrote it."},{"t":"Timeline · 117 AD The empire at its height","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> 117 AD The empire at its height Under Trajan, Rome rules from Britain to Mesopotamia. One law, one coinage, two literary languages."},{"t":"Timeline · 161–180 AD Marcus Aurelius' Meditations","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> 161–180 AD Marcus Aurelius' Meditations On campaign against the Marcomanni, the emperor writes his private notebook in Greek: Stoicism as a daily practice. Never meant for us — which is why it works."},{"t":"Timeline · 212 AD Universal citizenship","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"roman\"> 212 AD Universal citizenship Caracalla extends Roman citizenship to every free man in the empire. Law becomes the empire's true language."},{"t":"Timeline · 235–284 AD Crisis of the Third Century","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"late\"> 235–284 AD Crisis of the Third Century Fifty years of civil war, plague, and invasion nearly end the empire. Diocletian rebuilds it as an autocracy."},{"t":"Timeline · 313 AD Edict of Milan","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"late\"> 313 AD Edict of Milan Constantine legalizes Christianity. The empire's spiritual center of gravity begins to move."},{"t":"Timeline · 325 AD Council of Nicaea","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"late\"> 325 AD Council of Nicaea Bishops define orthodoxy by vote — rhetoric and logic turned to theology."},{"t":"Timeline · 410 AD The sack of Rome","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"late\"> 410 AD The sack of Rome Alaric's Goths take the city. Jerome writes: \"the city which had taken the whole world was itself taken.\""},{"t":"Timeline · 476 AD The Western Empire ends","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"late\"> 476 AD The Western Empire ends The traditional date: the last western emperor deposed. In the east, Rome continues another thousand years."},{"t":"Timeline · 529 AD The Academy closed","u":"timeline.html","s":"data-era=\"late\"> 529 AD The Academy closed Justinian shuts Plato's Academy after more than nine centuries. Antiquity ends; the texts go into monasteries — and wait for you. The New Academy · timeline"},{"t":"Review room — flashcards, drills, notes","u":"review.html","s":"Review room — The New Academy ◈ The New Academy Courses Review Library Timeline Study tools Review room The Greeks called it melete — care, practice, meditation. Return to what you've learned until it stays. Flashcards M"},{"t":"Search the academy","u":"search.html","s":"Search — The New Academy ◈ The New Academy Courses Review Library Timeline Study tools Search the academy Every lesson, every concept — find it in a keystroke. The New Academy · search"},{"t":"Final examinations","u":"exam.html","s":"Final exam — The New Academy ◈ The New Academy Courses Review Library Timeline Final examination Final exam The ancient schools examined their students before letting them advance. So do we. The New Academy · examination"},{"t":"Timeline of the classical world","u":"timeline.html","s":"Timeline — The New Academy ◈ The New Academy Courses Review Library Timeline Study tools Twelve centuries The classical world from the first Olympics to the closing of Plato's Academy — the stage on which everything you "},{"t":"The Library — primary sources","u":"library.html","s":"Library — The New Academy ◈ The New Academy Courses Review Library Timeline The Library Read the ancients themselves Commentary is a map; the text is the territory. Everything taught in the courses was drawn from these b"},{"t":"The New Academy — curriculum","u":"index.html","s":"The New Academy — A Modern Classical Education ◈ The New Academy Courses Review Library Timeline Paideia · for the modern world The New Academy The classical Greek and Roman curriculum rebuilt as a self-paced course for "}];
